  "worked_solution": "For a given numerical method, if $p$ is the largest positive integer such that\n\n- the global truncation error (GTE) satisfies\n\n$$\ne(\u0394x)=O\\,((\u0394x)^{p})\\,\\text{as}\\,\u0394x\\to 0,\n$$\n\n- the local truncation error (LTE) satisfies\n\n$$\n\u03c4(\u0394x)=O\\,((\u0394x)^{p+1})\\,\\text{as}\\,\u0394x\\to 0,\n$$\n\nthen the method is of order $p.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option in turn:\n\n- For the LTE expression $\u03c4(\u0394x)=5(\u0394x)^{4}+2(\u0394x)^{6},$ the dominant term as $\u0394x\\to 0$ is $5(\u0394x)^{4}=5(\u0394x)^{3+1}.$\nSo, statement I corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the GTE expression $e(\u0394x)=4(\u0394x)^{3}+7(\u0394x)^{4},$ the dominant term as $\u0394x\\to 0$ is $4(\u0394x)^{3}=4(\u0394x)^{3}.$\nSo, statement II corresponds to an order $3$ method.\n\n- For the LTE expression $\u03c4(\u0394x)=0.3(\u0394x)^{2}+6(\u0394x)^{3},$ the dominant term as $\u0394x\\to 0$ is $0.3(\u0394x)^{2}=0.3(\u0394x)^{1+1}.$\nSo, statement III corresponds to an order $1$ method.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
